#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1$." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= (-5t - 3)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{0} + (-5t - 3) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-3) \\cdot (-1) + (-8) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨q(t), q(t)⟩ &= q(0)q(0) + q(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= (t - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{0} + (t - 1) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-1) + 0 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{1} \\cdot (t - 1) \\\\\n&= 3(t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$, the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t$.\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V$:\n\n$f_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2$." :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) = \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t)\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2\n$$\n\nso let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}$. This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨g(t), f_{2}(t)⟩ &= ∫_{-1}^{1}g(t)f_{2}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}6t^{2} \\cdot \\sin (πt)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}6t^{2}\\sin (πt)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1]$, which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} 6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot \\sin \\pi t = 0\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}g(t) &= proj_{f_{1}(t)}g(t) + proj_{f_{2}(t)}g(t) \\\\\n&= 2 + 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733" :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= (p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1))/(q(0)^{2} + q(1)^{2}) \\cdot q(t) \\\\\n&= ((2)(0) + (6)(1))/(0^{2} + 1^{2}) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 6t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between\n\n$$\np(t) = 4t + 2\n$$\n\nand the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= ‖p(t) - proj_{q(t)}p(t)‖ \\\\\n&= ‖(4t + 2) - 6t‖ \\\\\n&= ‖ - 2t + 2‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩ &= (-2t + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (-2t + 2) & _{0} + (-2t + 2) & _{1} \\cdot (-2t + 2)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-2(0) + 2)^{2} + (-2(1) + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} + 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89410cb8-b3db-59ad-83ff-f84bc5b47061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130480} {:answer/id #